Next step: Check pod statuses. - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c0a484f5-6ab1-49ea-80ac-20f28728be65'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nHotel Reservation\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nhotel-reservation\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d466f689-6165-4cd3-a079-b7c09609e0c5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0634781e-b27a-406d-9c11-ac6831f63254'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. The goal is to monitor and diagnose any faults in the system. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster. Since the application is in the namespace 'hotel-reservation', I need to get a list of all the services within that namespace.\n\nLooking at the available tools, the 'get_services' tool from Grafana can retrieve the list of service names. However, the user mentioned that the application uses Kubernetes, so maybe checking the Kubernetes resources would be more direct. The 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' tool allows running kubectl commands. Using 'kubectl get services' in the 'hotel-reservation' namespace would list all the services. But the user might not have access to the cluster's kubectl, or maybe the Grafana tool is more appropriate here. Wait, the 'get_services' tool is specific to Grafana, which might not list all Kubernetes services but rather services monitored in Grafana. However, the user hasn't specified if Grafana is part of the setup. \n\nAlternatively, using 'get_services' from Grafana might give the list of services that are being monitored, which could be the same as the Kubernetes services. But if the application has many microservices, it's crucial to know all the services first. Since the user hasn't provided specific info, maybe starting with 'get_services' is better. But if that's not available, perhaps using kubectl. However, the 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' tool can run 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation' to list all services in that namespace. That would give the list of services, which is essential for further diagnosis. So, the first step is to get the list of services to identify which ones are part of the application. Therefore, the correct tool here is 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: To identify all microservices running in the hotel-reservation namespace, I need to first list all services (pods, deployments, etc.) in the cluster. Using kubectl get services in the target namespace will provide the foundational list of services to investigate further. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 496, 'prompt_tokens': 1500, 'total_tokens': 1996, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd06-bd4d-7ee2-801d-6caaadf46bca-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1500, 'output_tokens': 496, 'total_tokens': 1996}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='37c5b921-715d-44d4-9f05-a871fd47a55c'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me figure out the next step. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first thing I need to do is get a list of all the services running in the cluster, specifically in the hotel-reservation namespace. \n\nIn the previous step, I chose the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. The reasoning was that using kubectl get services in the target namespace would provide the list of services. But wait, the user mentioned that the application consists of many microservices, so maybe I should check for pods, deployments, and other resources as well. However, the initial step is to get the services, which are the network endpoints.\n\nWait, the user provided a list of tools. The get_services tool retrieves service names from Grafana, but maybe that's not the same as the Kubernetes services. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run kubectl commands. So, to get all services in the namespace, the command would be 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. But the user might also need pods, deployments, etc. However, the first step is to get the services. \n\nWait, the user's initial instruction says to get all pods and deployments. So maybe I should first list all pods and deployments. But the first tool call was to get services. Hmm. Let me check the available commands again. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows 'kubectl get' commands. So, to get all services, pods, deployments, etc., I need to run separate commands. \n\nBut the user's first step was to get the list of services from Grafana using get_services. However, the get_services tool's description says it retrieves service names from Grafana, which might not be the same as Kubernetes services. The user might be using Grafana for monitoring, so the service names in Grafana could correspond to Kubernetes services. But to be thorough, maybe I should first check the Kubernetes services using kubectl. \n\nWait, the user's initial problem is to monitor and diagnose the application, which has many microservices. The first step is to identify what services are running. So using kubectl to list all services, pods, deployments, etc., in the hotel-reservation namespace would be the right approach. \n\nSo, the first tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get all -n hotel-reservation' to get an overview of all resources. But the user might need to break it down into services, pods, deployments, etc. Alternatively, run separate commands for each. However, the user's initial instruction says to get all pods and deployments. \n\nWait, the user's first step in the thinking stage was to use get_traces, but the assistant chose exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. Then, the user's next step is to generate a tool call. So the previous step was to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. But maybe that's not sufficient. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct approach is to first list all services in the namespace. So the command would be 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. But the user might need to check for deployments as well. However, the initial step is to get the list of services. \n\nWait, the user's problem is to identify the microservices, so the first step is to list all services, pods, deployments, etc. So perhaps the correct command is 'kubectl get all -n hotel-reservation' to get all resources. But the available commands in the tool include 'kubectl get', so the command would be 'kubectl get all -n hotel-reservation'. \n\nBut the user's initial answer in the thinking stage was to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. However, the user might need to check for deployments as well. So maybe the next step is to run 'kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation' and 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation' to get all the deployments and pods. \n\nBut the first step is to get the list of services. So the correct tool call is to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. That would list all services in the namespace. Then, after that, proceed to check deployments and pods. \n\nSo the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c6e4c046eb470a5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 958, 'prompt_tokens': 2487, 'total_tokens': 3445, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd06-d4ca-79c0-928e-82ee9c505bcf-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c6e4c046eb470a5', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2487, 'output_tokens': 958, 'total_tokens': 3445}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     TYPE           CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP                                PORT(S)                               AGE\nconsul                   ClusterIP      10.100.83.36     <none>                                     8300/TCP,8400/TCP,8500/TCP,8600/UDP   109s\nfrontend                 ClusterIP      10.107.168.189   <none>                                     5000/TCP                              109s\ngeo                      ClusterIP      10.111.141.88    <none>                                     8083/TCP                              108s\njaeger                   ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                30s\njaeger-agent             ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                30s\njaeger-collector         ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                30s\njaeger-out               NodePort       10.108.43.229    <none>                                     16686:30182/TCP                       105s\njaeger-query             ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                30s\nmemcached-profile        ClusterIP      10.98.202.174    <none>                                     11211/TCP                             103s\nmemcached-rate           ClusterIP      10.101.117.50    <none>                                     11211/TCP                             100s\nmemcached-reserve        ClusterIP      10.110.13.85     <none>                                     11211/TCP                             91s\nmongodb-geo              ClusterIP      10.98.63.171     <none>                                     27017/TCP                             107s\nmongodb-profile          ClusterIP      10.96.145.123    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             102s\nmongodb-rate             ClusterIP      10.108.211.85    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             99s\nmongodb-recommendation   ClusterIP      10.107.151.190   <none>                                     27017/TCP                             96s\nmongodb-reservation      ClusterIP      10.106.67.100    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             88s\nmongodb-user             ClusterIP      10.96.106.140    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             80s\nprofile                  ClusterIP      10.111.33.134    <none>                                     8081/TCP                              101s\nrate                     ClusterIP      10.106.116.107   <none>                                     8084/TCP                              98s\nrecommendation           ClusterIP      10.111.202.2     <none>                                     8085/TCP                              94s\nreservation              ClusterIP      10.111.246.2     <none>                                     8087/TCP                              85s\nsearch                   ClusterIP      10.108.47.144    <none>                                     8082/TCP                              83s\nuser                     ClusterIP      10.110.240.137   <none>                                     8086/TCP                              76s\n', id='28557166-1d98-4971-8c53-40eaf8289aeb', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8c6e4c046eb470a5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d8f04521-8cf6-4101-bdc7-ad48266269a1'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step was to get the list of services using kubectl get services in the hotel-reservation namespace. The response showed a bunch of services like frontend, geo, memcached instances, MongoDB services, and others. \n\nNow, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty. Since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, I need to check for issues. The tools available include get_traces, get_services, get_operations, get_dependency_graph, get_metrics, and others. \n\nThe get_services tool from Grafana might help list the services, but the user already used kubectl to get services. Maybe the next step is to check the metrics for each service to see if there are any anomalies. Using get_metrics with PromQL queries could help identify high error rates, latency, or resource issues. Alternatively, checking the dependency graph with get_dependency_graph could show if there are any broken dependencies between services.\n\nAnother approach is to look at the traces using get_traces for specific services to see if there are any errors or slow transactions. But first, maybe it\'s better to get the list of services from Grafana to cross-verify with the kubectl list. Wait, the user already has the service names from kubectl. Maybe the next step is to check the metrics for each service. For example, checking the number of active pods, CPU/mem usage, or error rates. \n\nAlternatively, using get_operations to see what operations are available for each service in Grafana might give insights into their functionality. But without knowing which services are problematic, it\'s a bit vague. The get_dependency_graph could show which services are dependent on others, helping identify potential points of failure. \n\nConsidering that the user needs to find the root cause, starting with metrics might be more direct. So, using get_metrics to query for errors or latency in each service. For example, a query like "sum by (service) (count_over_time({job=\'otel-collector\', status=\'error\'}[5m]))" to check for errors. But the exact PromQL would depend on the metrics available. \n\nAlternatively, checking the number of pods for each service to see if they are in a healthy state. Using kubectl get deployments or replicasets might help, but the user has already listed services. Maybe the next step is to check the logs with kubectl logs, but the tool provided is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which can run logs. However, the user might need to specify which pod. \n\nWait, the user\'s initial step was to list services, so the next logical step is to check the metrics for each service. Let\'s choose get_metrics to query for any anomalies. For example, checking the number of active requests, error rates, or latency. So, the tool call would be get_metrics with a query that targets the services in the hotel-reservation namespace. \n\nAlternatively, using get_dependency_graph to visualize dependencies and see if any services are not communicating properly. But without knowing which services are faulty, it\'s hard to say. Maybe start with metrics to get a broad overview. So, the next tool would be get_metrics with a query that checks for errors or high latency across the services.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_metrics\nreasoning: To identify potential faults, I need to check real-time metrics for anomalies like high error rates, latency, or resource exhaustion. Using Prometheus queries, I can assess the health of each service by querying metrics such as error counts, request latency, and resource utilization (CPU/memory). This will help pinpoint services with abnormal behavior. \n\nNext, I\'ll query for error rates across all services in the `hotel-reservation` namespace.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 780, 'prompt_tokens': 2581, 'total_tokens': 3361, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-0381-7bf3-9cd4-c460030b16c2-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2581, 'output_tokens': 780, 'total_tokens': 3361}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='81e31184-90e1-4dc2-818c-66bbd3664e98'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose faults in a hotel reservation application with many microservices. They\'ve already used kubectl to list the services in the namespace, which gives a list of services like frontend, geo, memcached instances, MongoDBs, and others. Now they want to check metrics to find any anomalies.\n\nThe next step was to use the get_metrics tool. The reasoning was to check for error rates, latency, and resource usage. But the user hasn\'t specified a particular query yet. The tool requires a PromQL query as an argument. \n\nSo, I need to come up with a PromQL query that can detect errors across all services. Maybe start with a query that looks for HTTP error rates or grpc errors. Since the application uses gRPC, perhaps checking for grpc error counts. \n\nA common approach is to use the sum() function over a rate() of a metric that tracks errors. For example, sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) to get the error rate. But since the services might have different metrics, maybe a more general approach. \n\nAlternatively, check for high CPU or memory usage. But the user mentioned faults, which could be errors or latency. Let\'s focus on errors first. \n\nAnother thought: the services might have different names, so the query should be generic enough to cover all. Maybe using a query that looks for any errors in the namespace. However, without knowing the exact metric names, it\'s a bit tricky. \n\nWait, the user is in the hotel-reservation namespace. So maybe the metrics are labeled with the namespace. So a query like sum(rate(nginx_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) by (job) to check for errors. But again, not sure about the exact metric names. \n\nAlternatively, use a query that checks for high latency. For example, average(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{job="hotel-reservation"}[5m]) to see if any service has high latency. \n\nBut since the user hasn\'t provided specific metrics, maybe start with a general error rate query. Let\'s assume that there\'s a metric called "errors" or "http_errors". \n\nAlternatively, use the Prometheus query to check for all services in the namespace. Maybe the query could be something like:\n\nsum(rate(nginx_errors_total{job="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) by (job)\n\nBut since the exact metric names aren\'t known, perhaps the user should first check for common metrics. \n\nAlternatively, check for the number of active pods or their status. But that\'s more about availability than errors. \n\nWait, the user\'s previous step was to list the services, so now they need to check metrics for each service. Maybe the next step is to get the list of services first, then for each service, query its metrics. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. \n\nAlternatively, use a query that aggregates all services in the namespace. For example, sum(rate(nginx_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) by (job) to get the total requests and then check for errors. \n\nBut without knowing the exact metric names, it\'s challenging. Maybe the user should first check for the presence of any error metrics. \n\nAlternatively, use a query that checks for high CPU or memory usage across all pods in the namespace. For example:\n\nsum(rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{namespace="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) by (pod)\n\nBut that\'s more about resource exhaustion. \n\nHmm. The user\'s goal is to find faults, which could be errors, latency, or resource issues. Since the initial step was to list services, the next logical step is to check for errors. So, the best approach is to query for error rates. \n\nAssuming that the services expose metrics like http_requests_total or grpc_errors, the query could be:\n\nsum(rate(http_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation", status="5xx"}[5m])) by (job)\n\nBut again, without knowing the exact metric names, this is speculative. \n\nAlternatively, use a query that checks for any anomalies in the metrics. Maybe start with a general query to get an overview. \n\nIn the absence of specific metric names, perhaps the user should first check for the presence of any metrics related to errors. But since the tool requires a PromQL query, the assistant needs to make an educated guess. \n\nGiven that, the best approach is to query for error rates using a common metric name, like "errors" or "http_errors", and check for high values. \n\nSo, the tool call would be to get_metrics with a query like:\n\n"sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation", status!=\\"200\\"}[5m])) by (job)"\n\nBut since the exact metric names might vary, this is a best-effort approach. \n\nAlternatively, check for the number of active pods and their status. But that\'s more about availability. \n\nIn conclusion, the next step is to use get_metrics with a query that checks for error rates across all services in the namespace. The query should be generic enough to capture any errors, using common metric names like http_requests_total or similar.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"hotel-reservation\\", namespace=\\"hotel-reservation\\", status!=\\"200\\"}[5m])) by (job)"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb5bfd1f9f318832', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1155, 'prompt_tokens': 3580, 'total_tokens': 4735, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-2781-7352-ae75-af5b895a33a3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation", status!="200"}[5m])) by (job)'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb5bfd1f9f318832', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3580, 'output_tokens': 1155, 'total_tokens': 4735}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='4607f672-1fc5-40c3-98de-d83cf59e2cd1', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bb5bfd1f9f318832'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1e726617-c773-42ee-9ac1-943ffd0ab297')]]
