servation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they've checked the services and pods, and found that some pods are in an Init:0/1 state, meaning they're stuck during initialization. Also, some pods have restarts, like the profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, and user pods.\n\nThe next step is to check the logs of these problematic pods to find out why they're not starting properly. The user mentioned using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, which allows running kubectl commands. The specific command they need is to get logs for the custom-service pods and others with restarts.\n\nFirst, I should identify which pods are problematic. The custom-service pods are in Init:0/1, so their init containers might be failing. The other pods with restarts (profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, user) might have issues in their main containers. \n\nFor the custom-service pods, the init containers could be failing due to configuration errors, missing files, or failed setup scripts. Checking the logs of the init containers would help. The command would be kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation --previous to get logs from the init container.\n\nFor the other pods with restarts, the main container logs would show errors. Using kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation would retrieve those logs. Since some pods have multiple restarts, checking the latest logs would be essential.\n\nThe user needs to run these commands for each problematic pod. However, since the tool allows only one command per call, I should start with the custom-service pods first, as they are in an Init state, which is a critical issue. Then, proceed to check the other pods with restarts in subsequent steps.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --previous"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a3bae9cdcc8e070d', 'type': 'function