At: "2026-03-17T18:20:05Z"\n    volumeMounts:\n    - mountPath: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount\n      name: kube-api-access-vbljp\n      readOnly: true\n      recursiveReadOnly: Disabled\n  phase: Pending\n  podIP: 10.244.0.161\n  podIPs:\n  - ip: 10.244.0.161\n  qosClass: BestEffort\n  startTime: "2026-03-17T18:19:56Z"\n', id='a7171699-3708-489a-b17f-8dc1766c451c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-983471694b182eee'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d2e6a096-223b-4147-92ac-643525259cdc'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out what\'s going on with this custom-service pod. So, the pod is in a Pending state, and the init container hang-init is running sleep infinity. From the previous steps, I saw that the init container is stuck, which is preventing the main container from starting. \n\nLooking at the YAML spec, the init container\'s command is just sleeping indefinitely. That\'s odd. Why would an init container be set to sleep forever? Maybe it\'s a mistake in the deployment configuration. The main container\'s image is python:3.9-slim, and it\'s trying to run a HTTP server on port 8080. But if the init container isn\'t completing, the pod can\'t proceed to start the main container.\n\nWait, the init container\'s purpose is to run setup tasks before the main container starts. If it\'s just sleeping, maybe there\'s a missing step. Or perhaps the init container was meant to run a script that\'s not there. But the command is explicitly sleep infinity, which is a red flag. That\'s not a normal setup task. It\'s possible that the init container was incorrectly configured by someone, maybe as a placeholder or a test. \n\nThe user mentioned that the application includes a recommender system and reservation functionality. The custom-service might be part of that, but the name is a bit generic. Maybe the custom-service is supposed to handle some specific part of the application, but the init container is blocking it. \n\nSince the init container is stuck, the pod can\'t progress. The main container\'s status shows it\'s waiting because the pod is initializing. The root cause seems to be the init container\'s infinite sleep. The solution would be to fix the init container\'s command to perform the necessary setup tasks or remove it if it\'s not needed. \n\nBut how did this happen? Maybe during deployment, the init container\'s command was incorrectly set. The user might have intended to run a setup script but forgot to include it. Alternatively, it could be a leftover from a previous deployment. \n\nI need to confirm if there\'s any other configuration that might affect this. Checking the deployment or replica set for the custom-service could help. But since I can\'t use the get_operations or get_services tools here, maybe I should check the deployment YAML. However, the available tools don\'t include getting deployments directly. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is part of the deployment\'s spec. Since the user has access to kubectl, they can check the deployment\'s YAML. But in the current tools, the only way is through kubectl commands. \n\nWait, the user already used kubectl get pod -o yaml. The spec shows the init container\'s command. So the problem is definitely the init container\'s command. The root cause is that the init container is configured to sleep indefinitely, which prevents the pod from initializing and starting the main container. \n\nTherefore, the faulty component is the custom-service\'s init container with the sleep infinity command. The fix would involve correcting the init container\'s command to perform the necessary setup tasks or removing it if it\'s not required. \n\nSo, the final answer should state that the root cause is the init container in the custom-service pod being configured to sleep indefinitely, which blocks the pod from initializing and starting the main container.\n</think>\n\nsubmit_tool\n{"ans": "The root cause of the failure is the `custom-service` pod\'s init container (`hang-init`) being configured to run `sleep infinity`, which intentionally blocks the pod from initializing. This prevents the main container (`custom-service-main`) from starting, leaving the pod in a `Pending` state. The init container\'s infinite sleep suggests a misconfiguration or an intentional pause, likely due to an unimplemented setup task or a deployment error. The faulty component is the `hang-init` init container in the `custom-service` deployment, which must be corrected or removed to allow the pod to proceed with initialization."}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 806, 'prompt_tokens': 7352, 'total_tokens': 8158, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd08-79d7-7e30-95db-06f76496d450-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7352, 'output_tokens': 806, 'total_tokens': 8158}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='de617b1a-bdf2-46fe-8f74-ad1a0c090d2e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a Kubernetes cluster where a specific pod, custom-service, is stuck in a Pending state. From the previous interactions, I see that the pod\'s init container, hang-init, is running a sleep infinity command. That\'s probably why the pod isn\'t initializing properly.\n\nFirst, I need to confirm why the init container is set to sleep indefinitely. Init containers in Kubernetes are supposed to run before the main containers and set up any necessary conditions. If they\'re not completing, the main containers won\'t start. The user tried checking the logs for the init container, but since it\'s just sleeping, there\'s no output. \n\nLooking at the pod\'s YAML spec, the init container\'s command is indeed sleep infinity. That\'s a red flag. Normally, init containers have a specific task, like downloading files or setting up environment variables. If they\'re just sleeping, it\'s either a mistake or a deliberate pause. The user might have intended to have the init container perform some setup but forgot to add the actual commands. \n\nThe main container, custom-service-main, is using the python:3.9-slim image and running a simple HTTP server. But since the init container isn\'t completing, the main container can\'t start. The pod\'s status shows it\'s Pending because the init containers aren\'t done. \n\nThe user\'s last step was to submit the answer, identifying the hang-init init container as the root cause. The answer mentions that the infinite sleep is causing the issue. Now, the system is asking for a tool call after submitting. But since the user has already submitted the answer, maybe the process is done. However, the user might need to confirm if there\'s anything else to check. \n\nWait, the user used submit_tool, which should end the process. The system\'s response might be expecting a tool call, but since the answer was already submitted, perhaps there\'s no further action needed. The final answer should be the one provided, pointing out the init container\'s issue. I think the user has correctly identified the problem, so the submission is appropriate.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure is the `custom-service` pod\'s init container (`hang-init`) being configured to run `sleep infinity`, which intentionally blocks the pod from initializing. This prevents the main container (`custom-service-main`) from starting, leaving the pod in a `Pending` state. The init container\'s infinite sleep suggests a misconfiguration or an intentional pause, likely due to an unimplemented setup task or a deployment error. The faulty component is the `hang-init` init container in the `custom-service` deployment, which must be corrected or removed to allow the pod to proceed with initialization. \n\nThe issue has been diagnosed, and the faulty component has been identified. No further tool calls are required. \n\nsubmit_tool\n{"ans": "The root cause of the failure is the `custom-service` pod\'s init container (`hang-init`) being configured to run `sleep infinity`, which intentionally blocks the pod from initializing. This prevents the main container (`custom-service-main`) from starting, leaving the pod in a `Pending` state. The init container\'s infinite sleep suggests a misconfiguration or an intentional pause, likely due to an unimplemented setup task or a deployment error. The faulty component is the `hang-init` init container in the `custom-service` deployment, which must be corrected or removed to allow the pod to proceed with initialization."}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 715, 'prompt_tokens': 8386, 'total_tokens': 9101, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd08-a12c-7f30-9a8d-6120fcfd6e84-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8386, 'output_tokens': 715, 'total_tokens': 9101}), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='36d107b7-f6ae-435a-8e28-37a315503556')]]
