 Normal  Started    71s   kubelet            spec.initContainers{hang-init}: Started container hang-init\n', id='92ae9bbf-aaf9-404e-978c-12b7a6c2e9c7', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b4db9acd3629ab23'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='70d22a3a-b999-4212-877a-750f39131fad'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose the Hotel Reservation application, which has multiple microservices. The main issue seems to be with the custom-service pods. From the previous steps, the user ran kubectl get pods and found that the custom-service pods are in the Init:0/1 state, meaning they\'re stuck during initialization. Then, when trying to get logs, there was an error because the previous container wasn\'t found. \n\nNext, they used kubectl describe pod on one of the custom-service pods. The output shows that the pod is in a Pending state, and the Init Containers are running but not completing. The main container (custom-service-main) is waiting for initialization. The Events section shows that the init container (hang-init) was started, but there\'s no indication it\'s failing. The container is running a sleep infinity command, which would keep it alive indefinitely. \n\nWait, why is the pod still in Pending? The Init Containers are part of the pod\'s lifecycle. If the init containers don\'t complete, the pod won\'t proceed to start the main containers. In this case, the hang-init container is running but not exiting. The command is sleep infinity, which is a common way to keep a container running. But why isn\'t it completing? Maybe there\'s another init container that\'s missing or a step that\'s supposed to run before this. \n\nLooking at the pod\'s spec, there\'s only one init container, hang-init. The main container is waiting for the init containers to finish. Since hang-init is running but not exiting, the pod remains in Pending. The user needs to check why the init container isn\'t completing. The command is just sleeping, so maybe the init container is supposed to perform some setup that\'s not happening. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is designed to wait for a certain condition, but that condition isn\'t met. Or perhaps there\'s a missing init container that\'s supposed to run before hang-init. The user should check the pod\'s YAML definition to see the init containers\' commands and see if they\'re supposed to do anything beyond just sleeping. \n\nAnother angle: the pod\'s status shows Initialized as False, which means the init containers haven\'t completed. The main container\'s state is Waiting with reason PodInitializing, which aligns with the init containers not being done. The user might need to check the init container\'s logs to see if there\'s an error. However, the previous attempt to get logs for the previous container failed because it didn\'t exist. \n\nWait, the user tried to get logs for the previous container (custom-service-main) but got an error. Maybe the main container hasn\'t started yet because the init containers are still running. The init containers are the ones that need to complete first. So, the user should check the logs of the init container (hang-init) to see if there\'s any output. \n\nBut the hang-init container is running the command /bin/sh -c sleep infinity, which should just keep running without any output. Unless there\'s an error in the init container\'s setup. Maybe the init container is supposed to perform some checks or setup steps that are missing. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is part of a larger setup process, and the pod is waiting for something else. The user might need to look at the deployment or replica set for custom-service to see the init containers\' configurations. \n\nAnother possibility is that the init container is stuck because of a missing dependency or a configuration error. For example, if the init container is supposed to download some files or connect to a service, but that service isn\'t available yet. \n\nThe user should check the init container\'s logs. Since the hang-init container is running, they can try to get the logs for that specific init container. The command would be kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --container hang-init. That might show any output from the init container, which could indicate why it\'s not completing. \n\nIf there\'s no output, then the init container is just sleeping, which might be intentional. But why is the pod still in Pending? Maybe the init containers are supposed to run in a specific order, and one is missing. Or perhaps the main container\'s image isn\'t pulling correctly, but the user already checked the pod status and it\'s Pending, not ImagePullBackOff. \n\nAlternatively, the pod might be waiting for a node to be available, but the node is listed as node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net, which is scheduled. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the logs of the hang-init container to see if there\'s any error or output that explains why it\'s not completing. If there\'s no output, then the init container is just running indefinitely, which might be a misconfiguration. The user should then check the deployment or pod specification to see if the init containers are correctly defined and if there\'s any missing steps.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The pod is stuck in the "Pending" state with the init container "hang-init" running indefinitely. To diagnose why the init container isn\'t completing, I need to check its logs. The `hang-init` container is running a `sleep infinity` command, which suggests it\'s intentionally waiting, but this might indicate a missing setup step or a misconfigured init container. Retrieving its logs will confirm if there are any errors or outputs that explain the delay. \n\nNext step: Get logs for the "hang-init" init container.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1151, 'prompt_tokens': 5279, 'total_tokens': 6430, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-df51-7112-b385-bed6fcd010f9-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5279, 'output_tokens': 1151, 'total_tokens': 6430}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f5367257-97ed-4b63-929a-aea87c2bfcdc'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the custom-service pods are stuck in the Init:0/1 state. From the previous steps, they checked the pod description and found that the init container "hang-init" is running a sleep infinity command. The pod is in Pending status, and the main container isn\'t starting yet.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the logs of the init container to see if there\'s any output that might indicate why it\'s not proceeding. Since the init container is using a busybox image and running a shell command with sleep, there might be no output unless there\'s an error. But maybe the init container is supposed to perform some checks or setup that\'s not happening. \n\nThe user tried to get the logs of the previous container, but that didn\'t work. Now, they need to get the logs of the currently running init container. The command should be kubectl logs for the pod, specifying the init container\'s name. The init container\'s name is "hang-init" as per the describe output. \n\nSo the correct command would be kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --container hang-init. This will retrieve the logs from the init container, which might show any errors or outputs that explain why it\'s not completing. If there\'s no output, it might indicate that the init container is intentionally waiting, possibly due to a missing configuration or a deliberate pause, which could be part of a setup process that\'s not being triggered correctly. \n\nBy examining these logs, the user can determine if there\'s an error message or if the container is just waiting indefinitely, which would point to a configuration issue or a missing step in the init process.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --container hang-init"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-94437748d4347b32', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 410, 'prompt_tokens': 6305, 'total_tokens': 6715, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd08-15f3-7c00-8346-320608503653-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --container hang-init'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-94437748d4347b32', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6305, 'output_tokens': 410, 'total_tokens': 6715}), ToolMessage(content='', id='af98c30a-dd91-45ed-85fa-70d98e678d26', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-94437748d4347b32'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ddaf54cf-23ae-44a7-a3db-67078d06cdc2'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the custom-service pods in the hotel-reservation name