ypo or misconfiguration in the init container\'s command. \n\nAnother angle: the pod is in Pending state, which usually means it\'s waiting for resources. But the node is scheduled, so maybe it\'s not a resource issue. The init container might be waiting for a service that\'s not available, but without more info, it\'s hard to tell. \n\nThe next tool to use would be kubectl describe pod again to check the events and conditions. Wait, the user already did that. The events show the init container started but no errors. Maybe the init container is supposed to exit after completing a task, but it\'s stuck in an infinite loop. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is missing some environment variables or configurations that are necessary for it to proceed. But without more info, it\'s hard to say. \n\nSince the init container is using a sleep command, perhaps the user intended to have it wait for a certain condition, but that condition isn\'t met. However, without knowing what the init container is supposed to do, it\'s challenging to proceed. \n\nGiven the information, the next step is to check the pod\'s events again or look into the main container\'s logs. But the main container isn\'t starting yet. Maybe the init container is supposed to set up some files or services that the main container depends on. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is part of a larger setup process that\'s not being triggered. Since the user can\'t get logs from the init container, perhaps the issue is that the init container isn\'t configured to exit, leading to the pod being stuck. \n\nIn this case, the root cause might be that the init container is stuck in an infinite loop, preventing the main container from starting. The solution would be to fix the init container\'s command or configuration to ensure it completes successfully.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The init container `hang-init` is running `sleep infinity`, which is intentionally pr