e containers are failing to start or crashing. For example, if there\'s a configuration error, missing dependencies, or resource constraints. The user should check the logs for these specific pods to find the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the services are not properly communicating with each other. The dependency graph from Jaeger could show if there are any service disruptions or latency issues. But since the metrics didn\'t show errors, maybe the logs are the next best step. \n\nSo, the best tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the logs command for the problematic pods. That way, the user can see the actual error messages from the containers, which would point to the exact issue.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The "custom-service" pods are in Init:0/1 state, indicating they\'re stuck during initialization. Additionally, several pods have non-zero restart counts. To diagnose the root cause, I need to inspect their logs for errors during startup or runtime. This will reveal issues like configuration errors, missing dependencies, or failed container initialization. \n\nNext step: Retrieve logs for problematic pods.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 477, 'prompt_tokens': 3807, 'total_tokens': 4284, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-88b6-7831-9cd6-aab26702ed9b-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3807, 'output_tokens': 477, 'total_tokens': 4284}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1103cf0d-4f76-4780-8bec-e95607f77219'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the hotel reservation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they've checked the services and pods, and found that some pods are in an Init:0/1 state, meaning they're stuck during initialization. Also, some pods have restarts, like the profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, and user pods.\n\nThe next step is to check the logs of these problematic pods to find out why they're not starting properly. The user mentioned using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, which allows running kubectl commands. The specific command they need is to get logs for the custom-service pods and others with restarts.\n\nFirst, I should identify which pods are problematic. The custom-service pods are in Init:0/1, so their init containers might be failing. The other pods with restarts (profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, user) might have issues in their main containers. \n\nFor the custom-service pods, the init containers could be failing due to configuration errors, missing files, or failed setup scripts. Checking the logs of the init containers would help. The command would be kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation --previous to get logs from the init container.\n\nFor the other pods with restarts, the main container logs would show errors. Using kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation would retrieve those logs. Since some pods have multiple restarts, checking the latest logs would be essential.\n\nThe user needs to run these commands for each problematic pod. However, since the tool allows only one command per call, I should start with the custom-service pods first, as they are in an Init state, which is a critical issue. Then, proceed to check the other pods with restarts in subsequent steps.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --previous"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a3bae9cdcc8e070d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 423, 'prompt_tokens': 4802, 'total_tokens': 5225, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-9f30-7cb0-8f1e-4c3f9f6c89ca-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --previous'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a3bae9cdcc8e070d', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4802, 'output_tokens': 423, 'total_tokens': 5225}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nDefaulted container "custom-service-main" out of: custom-service-main, hang-init (init)\nError from server (BadRequest): previous terminated container "custom-service-main" in pod "custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7" not found\n', id='6d908e5b-a2c7-4f05-872b-b3092b10649c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a3bae9cdcc8e070d'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='46be8295-dc38-4e01-947d-d1d23c484194'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user tried to get the logs of the previous container for the custom-service pod, but there was an error. The error message says that the previous terminated container "custom-service-main" wasn\'t found. Hmm, maybe the pod hasn\'t restarted yet, or the container wasn\'t terminated before. Alternatively, maybe the pod is still in the process of initializing, so the previous container doesn\'t exist.\n\nSince the pod is in the Init:0/1 state, the initialization container might be stuck. The user tried to get logs from the main container, but the previous instance might not be available. Maybe I should check the current state of the pod\'s containers. Let me think about the available tools. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used to describe the pod to see more details about its containers and their statuses. That way, I can check if the init container is failing and get more information about why it\'s stuck. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user should check the logs of the init container instead. But the error message mentions the main container. Wait, the pod has two containers: custom-service-main and hang-init (init). The user tried to get logs for the main container\'s previous instance, which might not exist. So, perhaps first, I should describe the pod to see the current status of the containers and any error messages. That would help identify if the init container is failing, which could be the root cause. \n\nSo the next step is to use kubectl describe pod on the custom-service pod to get detailed information about the pod\'s status, events, and container statuses. This will help pinpoint why the init container is stuck and provide more clues about the issue. Let me choose the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with the describe command.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The error indicates the previous container "custom-service-main" is not found, likely because the pod hasn\'t restarted yet or the container wasn\'t terminated. To diagnose the init container issue, I need to inspect the pod\'s detailed status and events. Using `kubectl describe pod` will show container statuses, init container progress, and any error messages that explain why the pod is stuck in Init:0/1 state. This will help identify the root cause of the initialization failure. \n\nNext step: Describe the problematic pod.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 485, 'prompt_tokens': 4067, 'total_tokens': 4552, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-b623-7e00-8991-5c9377fd766f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4067, 'output_tokens': 485, 'total_tokens': 4552}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b2b8e6c8-3b40-44fd-9160-abbb5636a7d7'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the custom-service pods are stuck in Init:0/1 state. They tried getting logs with --previous, but that didn't work. Now they need to use kubectl describe pod to get more details.\n\nFirst, I should check the pod's description. The command would be kubectl describe pod custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation. This will show the pod's status, events, and container states. \n\nLooking at the output, the Init:0/1 state suggests that one of the init containers is failing. The describe command should list the init containers and any errors they encountered. Common issues could be missing files, permission problems, or failed commands in the init container. \n\nBy checking the events section, I can see if there are any error messages related to the init container. For example, if the init container is trying to perform a task like mounting a volume or running a script, and it's failing, the events would indicate that. \n\nOnce I have that information, I can determine the exact cause. If the init container is failing because a config file is missing, or a service isn't available, that's the root cause. Then, the user can address that specific issue, like ensuring the necessary files are present or checking service dependencies.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b4db9acd3629ab23', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 328, 'prompt_tokens': 5089, 'total_tokens': 5417, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-ccd2-7133-9824-3a067fef7e61-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b4db9acd3629ab23', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5089, 'output_tokens': 328, 'total_tokens': 5417}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7\nNamespace:        hotel-reservation\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net/128.110.96.31\nStart Time:       Tue, 17 Mar 2026 18:19:56 +0000\nLabels:           app=custom-service\n                  pod-template-hash=546dfb5d9\nAnnotations:      kubectl.kubernetes.io/restartedAt: 2026-03-17T18:19:53Z\nStatus:           Pending\nIP:               10.244.0.161\nIPs:\n  IP:           10.244.0.161\nControlled By:  ReplicaSet/custom-service-546dfb5d9\nInit Containers:\n  hang-init:\n    Container ID:  docker://4f2928e143df0976a95f4bb1e058a9e8ace58e1e21aea72a4f35b2f6fcc18728\n    Image:         busybox\n    Image ID:      docker-pullable://busybox@sha256:b3255e7dfbcd10cb367af0d409747d511aeb66dfac98cf30e97e87e4207dd76f\n    Port:          <none>\n    Host Port:     <none>\n    Command:\n      /bin/sh\n      -c\n      sleep infinity\n    State:          Running\n      Started:      Tue, 17 Mar 2026 18:20:05 +0000\n    Ready:          False\n    Restart Count:  0\n    Environment:    <none>\n    Mounts:\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-vbljp (ro)\nContainers:\n  custom-service-main:\n    Container ID:  \n    Image:         python:3.9-slim\n    Image ID:      \n    Port:          8080/TCP\n    Host Port:     0/TCP\n    Command:\n      python3\n      -m\n      http.server\n      8080\n    State:          Waiting\n      Reason:       PodInitializing\n    Ready:          False\n    Restart Count:  0\n    Environment:    <none>\n    Mounts:\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-vbljp (ro)\nConditions:\n  Type                        Status\n  PodReadyToStartContainers   True \n  Initialized                 False \n  Ready                       False \n  ContainersReady             False \n  PodScheduled                True \nVolumes:\n  kube-api-access-vbljp:\n    Type:                    Projected (a volume that contains injected data from multiple sources)\n    TokenExpirationSeconds:  3607\n    ConfigMapName:           kube-root-ca.crt\n    Optional:                false\n    DownwardAPI:             true\nQoS Class:                   BestEffort\nNode-Selectors:              <none>\nTolerations:                 node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason     Age   From               Message\n  ----    ------     ----  ----               -------\n  Normal  Scheduled  81s   default-scheduler  Successfully assigned hotel-reservation/custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 to node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  Normal  Pulling    77s   kubelet            spec.initContainers{hang-init}: Pulling image "busybox"\n  Normal  Pulled     73s   kubelet            spec.initContainers{hang-init}: Successfully pulled image "busybox" in 2.995s (3.773s including waiting). Image size: 2222260 bytes.\n  Normal  Created    72s   kubelet            spec.initContainers{hang-init}: Created container: hang-init\n  Normal  Started    71s   kubelet            spec.initContainers{hang-init}: Started container hang-init\n', id='92ae9bbf-aaf9-404e-978c-12b7a6c2e9c7', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b4db9acd3629ab23'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='70d22a3a-b999-4212-877a-750f39131fad'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose the Hotel Reservation application, which has multiple microservices. The main issue seems to be with the custom-service pods. From the previous steps, the user ran kubectl get pods and found that the custom-service pods are in the Init:0/1 state, meaning they\'re stuck during initialization. Then, when trying to get logs, there was an error because the previous container wasn\'t found. \n\nNext, they used kubectl describe pod on one of the custom-service pods. The output shows that the pod is in a Pending state, and the Init Containers are running but not completing. The main container (custom-service-main) is waiting for initialization. The Events section shows that the init container (hang-init) was started, but there\'s no indication it\'s failing. The container is running a sleep infinity command, which would keep it alive indefinitely. \n\nWait, why is the pod still in Pending? The Init Containers are part of the pod\'s lifecycle. If the init containers don\'t complete, the pod won\'t proceed to start the main containers. In this case, the hang-init container is running but not exiting. The command is sleep infinity, which is a common way to keep a container running. But why isn\'t it completing? Maybe there\'s another init container that\'s missing or a step that\'s supposed to run before this. \n\nLooking at the pod\'s spec, there\'s only one init container, hang-init. The main container is waiting for the init containers to finish. Since hang-init is running but not exiting, the pod remains in Pending. The user needs to check why the init container isn\'t completing. The command is just sleeping, so maybe the init container is supposed to perform some setup that\'s not happening. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is designed to wait for a certain condition, but that condition isn\'t met. Or perhaps there\'s a missing init container that\'s supposed to run before hang-init. The user should check the pod\'s YAML definition to see the init containers\' commands and see if they\'re supposed to do anything beyond just sleeping. \n\nAnother angle: the pod\'s status shows Initialized as False, which means the init containers haven\'t completed. The main container\'s state is Waiting with reason PodInitializing, which aligns with the init containers not being done. The user might need to check the init container\'s logs to see if there\'s an error. However, the previous attempt to get logs for the previous container failed because it didn\'t exist. \n\nWait, the user tried to get logs for the previous container (custom-service-main) but got an error. Maybe the main container hasn\'t started yet because the init containers are still running. The init containers are the ones that need to complete first. So, the user should check the logs of the init container (hang-init) to see if there\'s any output. \n\nBut the hang-init container is running the command /bin/sh -c sleep infinity, which should just keep running without any output. Unless there\'s an error in the init container\'s setup. Maybe the init container is supposed to perform some checks or setup steps that are missing. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is part of a larger setup process, and the pod is waiting for something else. The user might need to look at the deployment or replica set for custom-service to see the init containers\' configurations. \n\nAnother possibility is that the init container is stuck because of a missing dependency or a configuration error. For example, if the init container is supposed to download some files or connect to a service, but that service isn\'t available yet. \n\nThe user should check the init container\'s logs. Since the hang-init container is running, they can try to get the logs for that specific init container. The command would be kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --container hang-init. That might show any output from the init container, which could indicate why it\'s not completing. \n\nIf there\'s no output, then the init container is just sleeping, which might be intentional. But why is the pod still in Pending? Maybe the init containers are supposed to run in a specific order, and one is missing. Or perhaps the main container\'s image isn\'t pulling correctly, but the user already checked the pod status and it\'s Pending, not ImagePullBackOff. \n\nAlternatively, the pod might be waiting for a node to be available, but the node is listed as node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net, which is scheduled. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the logs of the hang-init container to see if there\'s any error or output that explains why it\'s not completing. If there\'s no output, then the init container is just running indefinitely, which might be a misconfiguration. The user should then check the deployment or pod specification to see if the init containers are correctly defined and if there\'s any missing steps.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The pod is stuck in the "Pending" state with the init container "hang-init" running indefinitely. To diagnose why the init container isn\'t completing, I need to check its logs. The `hang-init` container is running a `sleep infinity` command, which suggests it\'s intentionally waiting, but this might indicate a missing setup step or a misconfigured init container. Retrieving its logs will confirm if there are any errors or outputs that explain the delay. \n\nNext step: Get logs for the "hang-init" init container.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1151, 'prompt_tokens': 5279, 'total_tokens': 6430, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-df51-7112-b385-bed6fcd010f9-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5279, 'output_tokens': 1151, 'total_tokens': 6430}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f5367257-97ed-4b63-929a-aea87c2bfcdc'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the custom-service pods are stuck in the Init:0/1 state. From the previous steps, they checked the pod description and found that the init container "hang-init" is running a sleep infinity command. The pod is in Pending status, and the main container isn\'t starting yet.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the logs of the init container to see if there\'s any output that might indicate why it\'s not proceeding. Since the init container is using a busybox image and running a shell command with sleep, there might be no output unless there\'s an error. But maybe the init container is supposed to perform some checks or setup that\'s not happening. \n\nThe user tried to get the logs of the previous container, but that didn\'t work. Now, they need to get the logs of the currently running init container. The command should be kubectl logs for the pod, specifying the init container\'s name. The init container\'s name is "hang-init" as per the describe output. \n\nSo the correct command would be kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --container hang-init. This will retrieve the logs from the init container, which might show any errors or outputs that explain why it\'s not completing. If there\'s no output, it might indicate that the init container is intentionally waiting, possibly due to a missing configuration or a deliberate pause, which could be part of a setup process that\'s not being triggered correctly. \n\nBy examining these logs, the user can determine if there\'s an error message or if the container is just waiting indefinitely, which would point to a configuration issue or a missing step in the init process.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --container hang-init"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-94437748d4347b32', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 410, 'prompt_tokens': 6305, 'total_tokens': 6715, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd08-15f3-7c00-8346-320608503653-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation --container hang-init'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-94437748d4347b32', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6305, 'output_tokens': 410, 'total_tokens': 6715}), ToolMessage(content='', id='af98c30a-dd91-45ed-85fa-70d98e678d26', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-94437748d4347b32'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ddaf54cf-23ae-44a7-a3db-67078d06cdc2'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the custom-service pods in the hotel-reservation namespace. From the previous steps, we\'ve identified that the pods are stuck in the Init:0/1 state, and the init container hang-init is running indefinitely. The logs for hang-init didn\'t show any output, which is expected since it\'s just sleeping.\n\nNow, the next step is to figure out why the init container isn\'t completing. Since the init container is supposed to run before the main container starts, if it\'s stuck, the main container won\'t start. The user might have a custom init container that\'s supposed to perform some setup tasks, but it\'s not finishing. \n\nLooking at the pod description, the init container is using the busybox image and running a sleep infinity command. That\'s odd because normally, an init container would have a specific task, like checking for a file or waiting for a service. The sleep command here suggests that maybe the init container is supposed to wait for something but isn\'t configured correctly. \n\nSince the logs aren\'t helping, maybe the next step is to check the pod\'s events for any errors or messages that could indicate why the init container isn\'t proceeding. The events might show if there\'s a failure in the init container or if there\'s a problem with the pod\'s configuration. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is supposed to run a script or command that\'s not being executed. But since the command is just sleep, it\'s not doing anything. The user might have a typo or misconfiguration in the init container\'s command. \n\nAnother angle: the pod is in Pending state, which usually means it\'s waiting for resources. But the node is scheduled, so maybe it\'s not a resource issue. The init container might be waiting for a service that\'s not available, but without more info, it\'s hard to tell. \n\nThe next tool to use would be kubectl describe pod again to check the events and conditions. Wait, the user already did that. The events show the init container started but no errors. Maybe the init container is supposed to exit after completing a task, but it\'s stuck in an infinite loop. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is missing some environment variables or configurations that are necessary for it to proceed. But without more info, it\'s hard to say. \n\nSince the init container is using a sleep command, perhaps the user intended to have it wait for a certain condition, but that condition isn\'t met. However, without knowing what the init container is supposed to do, it\'s challenging to proceed. \n\nGiven the information, the next step is to check the pod\'s events again or look into the main container\'s logs. But the main container isn\'t starting yet. Maybe the init container is supposed to set up some files or services that the main container depends on. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is part of a larger setup process that\'s not being triggered. Since the user can\'t get logs from the init container, perhaps the issue is that the init container isn\'t configured to exit, leading to the pod being stuck. \n\nIn this case, the root cause might be that the init container is stuck in an infinite loop, preventing the main container from starting. The solution would be to fix the init container\'s command or configuration to ensure it completes successfully.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The init container `hang-init` is running `sleep infinity`, which is intentionally preventing it from completing. This suggests the init container is either misconfigured (e.g., missing a critical task) or intentionally paused for debugging. Since the pod is stuck in `Pending` and the init container isn\'t exiting, the root cause is likely the init container\'s infinite sleep, which blocks the main container from starting. This could indicate a missing setup step, a misconfigured init container, or an intentional pause.  \n\nNext step: Verify if the init container is supposed to run indefinitely or if there\'s a configuration error.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 870, 'prompt_tokens': 5507, 'total_tokens': 6377, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd08-2d45-7892-8754-80cbe80bd98b-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5507, 'output_tokens': 870, 'total_tokens': 6377}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8b0ac35d-1ec4-4b5f-8f6a-24ec6f729e99'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with a Kubernetes pod called custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 in the hotel-reservation namespace. From the previous steps, I can see that the pod is in a Pending state, and the init container hang-init is running indefinitely with a sleep infinity command. \n\nFirst, the user tried to get the logs of the hang-init container, but there were no logs because it's just sleeping. Then they described the pod again, which shows that the init container is running but hasn't completed. The main container, custom-service-main, is waiting for the init containers to finish, which they aren't.\n\nThe key here is why the init container isn't completing. The command in the init container is sleep infinity, which is a common way to pause a container indefinitely. But why is it there? Maybe the init container was supposed to perform some setup tasks, like downloading files, waiting for a service, or checking dependencies, but those tasks are missing. Alternatively, it might be a mistake in the deployment configuration where the init container was accidentally left to sleep forever.\n\nLooking at the pod's YAML configuration, if the hang-init container is supposed to run a script or command that should finish, but instead it's just sleeping, that's a problem. The user might need to check the init container's configuration to see if there's a missing step or an error in the command. \n\nSince the user can't modify the pod directly, the next step is to check the pod's specification to see what commands are defined in the init container. Using kubectl describe pod gives some info, but maybe the user needs to get the pod's YAML to see the exact commands and configurations. However, the available tools don't include a direct way to get the pod's YAML. Alternatively, maybe the user can check the deployment or daemonset that manages this pod to see the init container's configuration.\n\nWait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. They can use kubectl get pod with -o yaml to get the detailed configuration. But in the previous steps, the user tried kubectl describe pod, which already shows some details. The init container's command is listed as sleep infinity, which is part of the container's spec. \n\nSince the init container isn't completing, the root cause is that the init container's command is set to sleep indefinitely, which is preventing the main container from starting. This could be a configuration error where the init container was not properly set up to perform necessary tasks, or it's a deliberate pause for debugging. Either way, the pod will remain in Pending until the init container completes or the issue is resolved.\n\nThe user needs to check the init container's configuration to determine why it's set to sleep. If it's a mistake, correcting the command in the init container should allow it to finish and proceed to the main container. If it's intentional, then the user might need to adjust the setup or dependencies that the init container is supposed to handle.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pod custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-983471694b182eee', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 681, 'prompt_tokens': 6593, 'total_tokens': 7274, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd08-5679-73c3-919d-c5f446615153-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pod custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7 -n hotel-reservation -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-983471694b182eee', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6593, 'output_tokens': 681, 'total_tokens': 7274}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    kubectl.kubernetes.io/restartedAt: "2026-03-17T18:19:53Z"\n  creationTimestamp: "2026-03-17T18:19:55Z"\n  generateName: custom-service-546dfb5d9-\n  labels:\n    app: custom-service\n    pod-template-hash: 546dfb5d9\n  name: custom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7\n  namespace: hotel-reservation\n  ownerReferences:\n  - apiVersion: apps/v1\n    blockOwnerDeletion: true\n    controller: true\n    kind: ReplicaSet\n    name: custom-service-546dfb5d9\n    uid: a875bd02-6493-4a55-b500-f9275d01ae10\n  resourceVersion: "267113"\n  uid: cfd9ca40-39b3-47ff-a0d9-527e78ef9930\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - command:\n    - python3\n    - -m\n    - http.server\n    - "8080"\n    image: python:3.9-slim\n    imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent\n    name: custom-service-main\n    ports:\n    - containerPort: 8080\n      protocol: TCP\n    resources: {}\n    terminationMessagePath: /dev/termination-log\n  