eservation              ClusterIP      10.111.246.2     <none>                                     8087/TCP                              85s\nsearch                   ClusterIP      10.108.47.144    <none>                                     8082/TCP                              83s\nuser                     ClusterIP      10.110.240.137   <none>                                     8086/TCP                              76s\n', id='28557166-1d98-4971-8c53-40eaf8289aeb', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8c6e4c046eb470a5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d8f04521-8cf6-4101-bdc7-ad48266269a1'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step was to get the list of services using kubectl get services in the hotel-reservation namespace. The response showed a bunch of services like frontend, geo, memcached instances, MongoDB services, and others. \n\nNow, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty. Since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, I need to check for issues. The tools available include get_traces, get_services, get_operations, get_dependency_graph, get_metrics, and others. \n\nThe get_services tool from Grafana might help list the services, but the user already used kubectl to get services. Maybe the next step is to check the metrics for each service to see if there are any anomalies. Using get_metrics with PromQL queries could help identify high error rates, latency, or resource issues. Alternatively, checking the dependency graph with get_dependency_graph could show if there are any broken dependencies between services.\n\nAnother approach is to look at the traces using get_traces for specific services to see if there are any errors or slow transactions. But first, maybe it\'s better to get the list of services from Grafana to cross-verify with the kubectl list. Wait, the user already has the service names from kubectl. Maybe the next step is to check the metrics for each service. For example, checking the number of active pods, CPU/mem usage, or error rates. \n\nAlternatively, using get_operations to see what operations are available for each service in Grafana might give insights into their functionality. But without knowing which services are problematic, it\'s a bit vague. The get_dependency_graph could show which services are dependent on others, helping identify potential points of failure. \n\nConsidering that the user needs to find the root cause, starting with metrics might be more direct. So, using get_metrics to query for errors or latency in each service. For example, a query like "sum by (service) (count_over_time({job=\'otel-collector\', status=\'error\'}[5m]))" to check for errors. But the exact PromQL would depend on the metrics available. \n\nAlternatively, checking the number of pods for each service to see if they are in a healthy state. Using kubectl get deployments or replicasets might help, but the user has already listed services. Maybe the next step is to check the logs with kubectl logs, but the tool provided is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which can run logs. However, the user might need to specify which pod. \n\nWait, the user\'s initial step was to list services, so the next logical step is to check the metrics for each service. Let\'s choose get_metrics to query for any anomalies. For example, checking the number of active requests, error rates, or latency. So, the tool call would be get_metrics with a query that targets the services in the hotel-reservation namespace. \n\nAlternatively, using get_dependency_graph to visualize dependencies and see if any services are not communicating properly. But without knowing which services are faulty, it\'s hard to say. Maybe start with metrics to get a broad overview. So, the next tool would be get_metrics with a query that checks for errors or high latency across the services.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_metrics\nreasoning: To identify potential faults, I need to check real-time metrics for anomalies like high error rates, latency, or resource exhaustion. Using Prometheus queries, I can assess the health of each service by querying metrics such as error counts, request latency, and resource utilization (CPU/memory). This will help pinpoint services with abnormal behavior. \n\nNext, I\'ll query for error rates across all services in the `hotel-reservation` namespace.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 780, 'prompt_tokens': 2581, 'total_tokens': 3361, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-0381-7bf3-9cd4-c460030b16c2-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2581, 'output_tokens': 780, 'total_tokens': 3361}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='81e31184-90e1-4dc2-818c-66bbd3664e98'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose faults in a hotel reservation application with many microservices. They\'ve already used kubectl to list the services in the namespace, which gives a list of services like frontend, geo, memcached instances, MongoDBs, and others. Now they want to check metrics to find any anomalies.\n\nThe next step was to use the get_metrics tool. The reasoning was to check for error rates, latency, and resource usage. But the user hasn\'t specified a particular query yet. The tool requires a PromQL query as an argument. \n\nSo, I need to come up with a PromQL query that can detect errors across all services. Maybe start with a query that looks for HTTP error rates or grpc errors. Since the application uses gRPC, perhaps checking for grpc error counts. \n\nA common approach is to use the sum() function over a rate() of a metric that tracks errors. For example, sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) to get the error rate. But since the services might have different metrics, maybe a more general approach. \n\nAlternatively, check for high CPU or memory usage. But the user mentioned faults, which could be errors or latency. Let\'s focus on errors first. \n\nAnother thought: the services might have different names, so the query should be generic enough to cover all. Maybe using a query that looks for any errors in the namespace. However, without knowing the exact metric names, it\'s a bit tricky. \n\nWait, the user is in the hotel-reservation namespace. So maybe the metrics are labeled with the namespace. So a query like sum(rate(nginx_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) by (job) to check for errors. But again, not sure about the exact metric names. \n\nAlternatively, use a query that checks for high latency. For example, average(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{job="hotel-reservation"}[5m]) to see if any service has high latency. \n\nBut since the user hasn\'t provided specific metrics, maybe start with a general error rate query. Let\'s assume that there\'s a metric called "errors" or "http_errors". \n\nAlternatively, use the Prometheus query to check for all services in the namespace. Maybe the query could be something like:\n\nsum(rate(nginx_errors_total{job="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) by (job)\n\nBut since the exact metric names aren\'t known, perhaps the user should first check for common metrics. \n\nAlternatively, check for the number of active pods or their status. But that\'s more about availability than errors. \n\nWait, the user\'s previous step was to list the services, so now they need to check metrics for each service. Maybe the next step is to get the list of services first, then for each service, query its metrics. But the user hasn\'t done that yet. \n\nAlternatively, use a query that aggregates all services in the namespace. For example, sum(rate(nginx_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) by (job) to get the total requests and then check for errors. \n\nBut without knowing the exact metric names, it\'s challenging. Maybe the user should first check for the presence of any error metrics. \n\nAlternatively, use a query that checks for high CPU or memory usage across all pods in the namespace. For example:\n\nsum(rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{namespace="hotel-reservation"}[5m])) by (pod)\n\nBut that\'s more about resource exhaustion. \n\nHmm. The user\'s goal is to find faults, which could be errors, latency, or resource issues. Since the initial step was to list services, the next logical step is to check for errors. So, the best approach is to query for error rates. \n\nAssuming that the services expose metrics like http_requests_total or grpc_errors, the query could be:\n\nsum(rate(http_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation", status="5xx"}[5m])) by (job)\n\nBut again, without knowing the exact metric names, this is speculative. \n\nAlternatively, use a query that checks for any anomalies in the metrics. Maybe start with a general query to get an overview. \n\nIn the absence of specific metric names, perhaps the user should first check for the presence of any metrics related to errors. But since the tool requires a PromQL query, the assistant needs to make an educated guess. \n\nGiven that, the best approach is to query for error rates using a common metric name, like "errors" or "http_errors", and check for high values. \n\nSo, the tool call would be to get_metrics with a query like:\n\n"sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation", status!=\\"200\\"}[5m])) by (job)"\n\nBut since the exact metric names might vary, this is a best-effort approach. \n\nAlternatively, check for the number of active pods and their status. But that\'s more about availability. \n\nIn conclusion, the next step is to use get_metrics with a query that checks for error rates across all services in the namespace. The query should be generic enough to capture any errors, using common metric names like http_requests_total or similar.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"hotel-reservation\\", namespace=\\"hotel-reservation\\", status!=\\"200\\"}[5m])) by (job)"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb5bfd1f9f318832', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1155, 'prompt_tokens': 3580, 'total_tokens': 4735, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-2781-7352-ae75-af5b895a33a3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="hotel-reservation", namespace="hotel-reservation", status!="200"}[5m])) by (job)'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb5bfd1f9f318832', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3580, 'output_tokens': 1155, 'total_tokens': 4735}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='4607f672-1fc5-40c3-98de-d83cf59e2cd1', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bb5bfd1f9f318832'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1e726617-c773-42ee-9ac1-943ffd0ab297'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose faults in the hotel reservation application, which has many microservices. So far, they\'ve used kubectl to list the services in the namespace and checked metrics for error rates. The metrics query returned an empty result, which might mean there are no errors reported in the last 5 minutes, or maybe the query isn\'t correctly targeting the right metrics.\n\nHmm, maybe the error rate query isn\'t the right approach. Let me think. If there are no errors detected, perhaps the issue is something else, like high latency or resource constraints. Alternatively, maybe the metrics aren\'t being collected properly for some services. \n\nAnother angle: the user listed services using kubectl, but that only shows the services, not their current status or health. Maybe checking the pods\' status would help. If some pods are crashing or not running, that could be a root cause. So using kubectl to check pod statuses in the namespace could reveal if any pods are in a bad state.\n\nWait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. They could run "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" to see the pod statuses. That would show if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or similar states. That\'s a good next step because it\'s a direct way to check if any microservices are not running properly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe checking the deployments to see if they\'re scaled down or have issues. But first, checking the pods would be more immediate. If pods are not running, that\'s a clear sign of a problem. So the next tool call should be to get the pods in the namespace.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The previous metrics query for errors returned no results, suggesting either no errors or potential misconfiguration in the metric query. To directly check the health of microservices, I will now inspect the pod statuses in the `hotel-reservation` namespace. This will reveal if any pods are in a failed state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error) or not running, which could indicate underlying issues like resource exhaustion, startup failures, or deployment problems. \n\nNext step: Check pod statuses.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 456, 'prompt_tokens': 2803, 'total_tokens': 3259, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-630c-7562-b919-f07b602796d1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2803, 'output_tokens': 456, 'total_tokens': 3259}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a26e66b9-3dab-4637-a06a-69a120fb8d86'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. So far, they\'ve used kubectl to list services and checked metrics for errors, but the metrics query returned no results. Now, they want to check pod statuses to see if any pods are failing.\n\nThe next logical step is to use kubectl to get pod information in the hotel-reservation namespace. The command would be something like "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation". This will show the current state of all pods, including any that are not running or have errors. \n\nBy examining the output, they can look for pods in a CrashLoopBackOff state, which indicates repeated failures, or pods that are Pending, which might mean resource constraints or image pull issues. Also, checking the number of replicas and their status can help identify if deployments are not scaling correctly or if there are issues with the underlying nodes.\n\nThis information will help narrow down the problem to specific pods or services that might be causing the overall application issues. If any pods are problematic, further steps like checking logs or resource usage would be necessary. But first, getting the pod statuses is crucial to identify which components are not functioning as expected.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9aaa6e465111b7d2', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 284, 'prompt_tokens': 3819, 'total_tokens': 4103, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd07-7895-7801-916d-03a2683e17b0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9aaa6e465111b7d2', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3819, 'output_tokens': 284, 'total_tokens': 4103}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS     RESTARTS       AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-6gt9h                   1/1     Running    0              2m23s\ncustom-service-546dfb5d9-bszs7            0/1     Init:0/1   0              60s\ncustom-service-546dfb5d9-dmznd            0/1     Init:0/1   0              60s\ncustom-service-546dfb5d9-jm78n            0/1     Init:0/1   0              60s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-nhtwz                 1/1     Running    0              2m23s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-2w2s6                      1/1     Running    1 (2m3s ago)   2m23s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-s6465                   1/1     Running    0              2m20s\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-nkbgx         1/1     Running    0              2m17s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-mtwx5            1/1     Running    0              2m15s\nmemcached-res