leep suggests a misconfiguration or an intentional pause, likely due to an unimplemented setup task or a deployment error. The faulty component is the `hang-init` init container in the `custom-service` deployment, which must be corrected or removed to allow the pod to proceed with initialization."}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 464, 'prompt_tokens': 11093, 'total_tokens': 11557, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd0a-494e-7150-9169-d39c2ff5bdb1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11093, 'output_tokens': 464, 'total_tokens': 11557}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='110d6a06-bda0-4095-9602-6f8171b513cb'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a Kubernetes cluster where a specific pod, custom-service, is stuck in a Pending state. The problem seems to be with the init container called hang-init, which is running a sleep infinity command. \n\nFirst, I need to recall the tools available. There\'s kubectl, which can be used to get pod details, describe pods, check logs, etc. The user has already tried several commands: getting the list of services, checking metrics, looking at pod statuses, and describing the pod. They found that the init container is stuck, and the main container isn\'t starting because of it.\n\nThe key issue here is the hang-init container. The command sleep infinity is causing the init container to never complete, which in turn prevents the main container from starting. The user has already checked the pod\'s YAML and confirmed the init container\'s configuration. Since the i